    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d106-a16">

                <pb facs="0257" n="201" sameAs="frus:frus1876/pg201"/>

                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Liverpool</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1875-04-01"><hi rendition="#italic">April</hi> 1,
                        1875</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Gentlemen</hi>: In reference to the signals proposed
                    by the board of trade in foggy misty, or snowy weather, I understand the signals
                    A, B, and C are compulsory, but in my opinion the interval between each
                    prolonged blast is too long; I think it ought to be made at least once every two
                    minutes, or even every minute.</p>
                <p>With regard to the additional signals, I think one short blast, “I am porting;”
                    two short blasts, “I am starboarding;” and three short blasts, “I am going
                    astern,” are quite sufficient; I do not see the use of the signal, “I am taking
                    care,” as each vessel should at all times be doing so.</p>
                <p>I am, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>W. MASON,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Second Officer Steamship
                            Republic</hi>.</signed></closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p>Messrs. <hi rendition="#small-caps">Ismay, Imrie</hi> &amp; Co.</p>
                </postscript>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
